@using MartenStudio.Services

@* The two pickers a person sets once and then leaves alone.

   They are behind a gear rather than in the header row because at 390 px the header was four rows
   deep before the page title appeared: the tenant picker, the capability chip, a time-zone select
   wide enough to hold "FLE Standard Time (local)", and a theme select. Only the first of those is
   context somebody needs while they read the page - which store, which database, which tenant this
   screen is about - so that is the one that stays, and these two move.

   One behaviour at every width, deliberately. A control that is in the header on a laptop and behind
   a gear on a phone is two things to learn and two shapes to test; this is one.

   The popover's contents are rendered whatever its state. The `popover` attribute hides them with the
   user agent's own `display: none`, which is what a browser - and Playwright - operates against, and
   rendering them only while open would mean the selects do not exist until something has clicked the
   button, and would rebuild them over the circuit on every open. *@

<button type="button"
        class="ms-button ms-btn-icon ms-preferences-button"
        popovertarget="ms-preferences-popover"
        aria-controls="ms-preferences-popover"
        aria-expanded="@(open ? "true" : "false")"
        aria-label="Preferences"
        title="Preferences: theme, time zone">
    @* Hand-drawn, like every other icon here: no icon library, ever (AGENTS.md hard rule 3).

       A toothed cog, and deliberately not the circle-and-spokes glyph NavMenu draws for
       Configuration: that one reads as a *sun* at 18 px - which is the worst thing to put on the
       button that opens the theme picker, since it looks like it says "light" - and it is already
       taken, by a nav entry three centimetres away. Six square teeth about (9, 9), outer radius 8,
       root 5.4, each tooth 32 degrees wide with a radial drop into a 28 degree gap. Six and not
       eight: at this size eight teeth close up into a ring. *@
    <svg class="ms-preferences-icon" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
         stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M16.69 6.79 16.69 11.21 14.19 10.49 12.88 12.75 14.75 14.56 10.94 16.76 10.31 14.24 7.69 14.24 7.06 16.76 3.25 14.56 5.12 12.75 3.81 10.49 1.31 11.21 1.31 6.79 3.81 7.51 5.12 5.25 3.25 3.44 7.06 1.24 7.69 3.76 10.31 3.76 10.94 1.24 14.75 3.44 12.88 5.25 14.19 7.51 Z" />
        <circle cx="9" cy="9" r="2.5" />
    </svg>
</button>

@* Native popover, anchored to the button above: no positioning library and no third-party menu
   component, the same mechanism CapabilityBanner already uses. *@
<div id="ms-preferences-popover"
     popover="auto"
     class="ms-popover ms-preferences-popover"
     @ontoggle="OnToggled">
    <p class="ms-popover-title">Preferences</p>

    @* Every picker carries its own `aria-label` as well as its `<label for>`. The label is painted
       here - a popover has room the header did not - but the `aria-label` stays, because it is the
       accessible name no stylesheet can take away and these two ids are what the browser suite and
       the component tests drive. *@
    <div class="ms-header-control ms-preferences-control">
        <label class="ms-header-control-label" for="ms-timezone-select">Time zone</label>
        <select id="ms-timezone-select"
                class="ms-select ms-header-select"
                aria-label="Time zone"
                value="@SelectedTimeZoneId"
                @onchange="TimeZoneChanged">
            @foreach (TimeZoneInfo timeZone in timeZones)
            {
                <option value="@timeZone.Id" selected="@(timeZone.Id == SelectedTimeZoneId)">@TimeZoneLabel(timeZone)</option>
            }
        </select>
    </div>

    <div class="ms-header-control ms-preferences-control">
        <label class="ms-header-control-label" for="ms-theme-select">Theme</label>
        <select id="ms-theme-select"
                class="ms-select ms-header-select"
                aria-label="Theme"
                value="@SelectedTheme"
                @onchange="ThemeChanged">
            <option value="system" selected="@(SelectedTheme == "system")">System</option>
            <option value="light" selected="@(SelectedTheme == "light")">Light</option>
            <option value="dark" selected="@(SelectedTheme == "dark")">Dark</option>
        </select>
    </div>

    <p class="ms-popover-footnote">
        Both are remembered by this browser alone. Neither changes what the studio reads, only how it
        is drawn and which zone its timestamps are rendered in.
    </p>
</div>

@code {
    private readonly IReadOnlyList<TimeZoneInfo> timeZones = TimeZoneInfo
        .GetSystemTimeZones()
        .OrderBy(static tz => tz.Id, StringComparer.Ordinal)
        .ToList();

    private bool open;

    /// <summary><c>system</c>, <c>light</c> or <c>dark</c>, as the shell currently has it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string SelectedTheme { get; set; } = "system";

    /// <summary>The time zone every timestamp in the studio is rendered in.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string SelectedTimeZoneId { get; set; } = TimeZoneInfo.Utc.Id;

    /// <summary>Raised with the picked theme.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The handlers stay in <c>StudioLayout</c> rather than moving here with the markup. They write
    /// the browser's stored preference and they are one half of the race that
    /// <c>StudioState.PreferenceVersion</c> exists to settle - a visitor who moves a picker while the
    /// first-render read of <c>localStorage</c> is still in flight - and that logic belongs with the
    /// read it is racing, which is the layout's.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<ChangeEventArgs> ThemeChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised with the picked time zone id.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<ChangeEventArgs> TimeZoneChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Keeps <c>aria-expanded</c> honest.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// A popover closes four ways - the button again, Escape, a click outside it, and another popover
    /// opening - and only one of them is a click on anything this component renders. The platform's
    /// own <c>toggle</c> event is the single signal that covers all four, so it is what the attribute
    /// is driven from; a button-click handler would say "expanded" to a screen reader long after the
    /// menu had been dismissed with Escape.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// It flips rather than reads a state, because Blazor surfaces <c>ontoggle</c> as a plain
    /// <see cref="EventArgs" /> with no <c>newState</c> on it. That is exact: the event fires once per
    /// state change and the popover starts closed.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Synchronous, and deliberately not <c>async void</c> under another name (AGENTS.md hard rule 6):
    /// there is nothing here to await.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private void OnToggled() => open = !open;

    private static string TimeZoneLabel(TimeZoneInfo timeZone)
    {
        return string.Equals(timeZone.Id, TimeZoneInfo.Local.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal)
            ? timeZone.Id + " (local)"
            : timeZone.Id;
    }
}
